= Migrating from crent370 and libc370 1.x <pg-migrate>

#idx("migration", "from libc370 1.x")#idx("crent370")
libc370 2.0 reorganized the public headers. The functions, their behavior
and the code a program links are, with a short list of exceptions, what
1.x had -- but almost every source that includes a library header has to
change its #cmd("#include") lines. This appendix says what changed, how to
stay on 1.x until you are ready, and how to migrate one source file at a
time.

2.0 is a hard cut: there are no compatibility headers and no deprecated
names. An include of 1.x either resolves to its 2.0 header or does not
compile.

== Coming from crent370 <pg-migrate-crent>

crent370 is the name under which the library was created, before it became
the library of the cc370 toolchain and was renamed libc370. A program built
against crent370 uses the header names of libc370 1.x and migrates as a 1.x
program, as described in the rest of this appendix. In addition:

- The library is no longer a dependency of the project. It is installed in
  the sysroot of cc370, which finds its headers without #cmd("-I") and
  links #cmd("libc.a") with #cmd("-lc") (@pg-intro-install). Remove a
  dependency on crent370 from the build.
- libc370 2.1 and later need cc370 1.1.0 or later\; every header stops
  with #cmd("#error") under an older compiler.
- Assembler source that copies #cmd("PDPMAIN") was generated by a
  compiler before cc370. The macros #cmd("PDPMAIN"), #cmd("PDP370"),
  #cmd("PDP380"), #cmd("PDP390") and #cmd("PDPORIG") are not shipped any
  more: compile the C source again with cc370.

== Staying on 1.x <pg-migrate-stay>

The last 1.x release is 1.0.8. Its tag #cmd("v1.0.8") and the branch
#cmd("1.x") remain in the libc370 repository.

- In an mbt project, declare #cmd("libc370 = \"1.0.8\"") in the
  #cmd("[toolchain]") table of #cmd("project.toml").
- Installed by hand: #cmd("git checkout v1.0.8") and #cmd("make install").

#cmd("make install") installs into the sysroot of the first cc370 on the
#cmd("PATH") and _replaces_ the headers there. To keep both, install the
toolchain twice under two prefixes, each with its own libc370, and build each
project with the #cmd("PATH") it needs. mbt refuses a sysroot older than the
#cmd("[toolchain]") pin of a project, so a wrong one is noticed.

== What Changed in 2.0 <pg-migrate-changes>

- *The headers moved into a tree:* #cmd("mvs/") for the MVS run time and
  services, #cmd("ext/") for portable extras (dynamic arrays, 64-bit
  helpers, time beyond 2038), #cmd("ibm/") for IBM control blocks, data
  layout only, #cmd("s370/") for the architecture, and the POSIX headers
  #cmd("<sys/socket.h>"), #cmd("<netinet/in.h>"), #cmd("<arpa/inet.h>"),
  #cmd("<strings.h>"), #cmd("<unistd.h>") and others. Headers that mixed
  purposes were _split_\; internal ones left the sysroot.
- *Names:* 880 of the 5481 public names of 1.x are gone from the public
  headers, nearly all of them declared and never built, or internal. The
  few a program can have used have a replacement (@pg-migrate-removed).
- *Cryptography* -- SHA-256, Blowfish and base64 -- moved to the separate
  library crypto370. In an mbt project, add
  #cmd("\"mvslovers/crypto370\" = \">=1.0.0\"") to #cmd("[dependencies]").
  #cmd("<clibb64.h>") is #cmd("<base64.h>") there\; the C names did not
  change, the external names did (#cmd("B64ENC"), #cmd("B64DEC")).
- *A few interfaces changed their meaning* (@pg-migrate-meaning).
- The library itself is compiled as C99 (#cmd("-std=gnu99")).

