For a split header, include only the headers that declare the names the
source actually uses. The procedure below finds them.

== Migrating a Source File <pg-migrate-procedure>

#idx("migration", "procedure")
The libc370 source repository keeps three maps in its directory
#cmd("sdk/"), tab-separated text with one row per entry:

#deflist(width: 1.5in,
  [#cmd("headermap.tsv")], [one row per 1.x header: where it went. The
    target is a path under #cmd("include/") (the header moved), the same
    name (an ISO C header, unchanged), #cmd("split"), #cmd("internal") (it
    left the sysroot) or #cmd("removed").],
  [#cmd("names.tsv")], [one row per public name of 2.0: the header or
    headers that declare it.],
  [#cmd("removed.tsv")], [one row per 1.x name that no public header
    declares any more, with what to use instead.],
)

For each source file:

+ *Collect* the library headers the file includes and the identifiers it
  uses, leaving out comments and string literals.
+ *Rewrite each include* by its row in #cmd("headermap.tsv"). A moved
  header is replaced by its new name\; an unchanged ISO header stays\; a
  #cmd("split"), #cmd("internal") or #cmd("removed") header is dropped, and
  the next step supplies what the file needs from it.
+ *Resolve by name.* For every identifier the file uses that
  #cmd("names.tsv") lists, make sure the file includes one of its headers --
  the one it includes already, if any, otherwise the first one listed. This
  also catches names the file only saw through another header: the 1.x
  #cmd("socket.h") pulled in every socket header, the 2.0
  #cmd("<mvs/socket.h>") does not.
+ *Replace removed names* by their row in #cmd("removed.tsv")
  (@pg-migrate-removed).
+ *Keep the delimiters:* library headers are included as
  #cmd("<")...#cmd(">").

A header of the program's own that has the same base name as a library
header is not the library's: resolve includes against the program's own
tree first.

